            {
              "id": "a5927527-34de-320d-9d50-301231574dcf",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. In the provided JavaScript code, the value of an HTML element is directly controlled by the user, which can lead to an XSS attack if the user input is not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always sanitize user input before using it in your code. This can be done by using a library that provides functions for sanitizing HTML strings, or by implementing your own sanitization function. Additionally, you should use textContent property instead of innerHTML when setting the text of an HTML element to prevent the browser from interpreting the text as HTML.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nlet element = document.getElementById('first_name_' + id);\nlet user_input = element.value;\nelement.textContent = user_input;\n```\n\nIn this code, the textContent property is used instead of innerHTML, which prevents the browser from interpreting the user input as HTML.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4b61b340-44c2-36c1-97ba-d367b5819328",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string exists when an application includes untrusted data in a new HTML document without proper validation or escaping. This allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `id` variable is directly concatenated into the HTML string which is then used to get the value of an element. If the `id` variable is controlled by the user and not properly sanitized, it can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Validate Input:** Ensure that all user-supplied input is validated before it is processed. Use a whitelist of allowed inputs rather than trying to filter out bad inputs.\n\n2. **Escape Untrusted Data:** If user data is inserted into HTML, JavaScript, CSS, or other web content, it should be properly escaped to prevent it from being interpreted as code.\n\n3. **Use Safe APIs:** Some APIs provide automatic escaping to prevent XSS vulnerabilities. For example, the `textContent` property in JavaScript automatically escapes HTML tags.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer version of the provided code would look like this:\n\n```javascript\nlet elementId = 'surname_' + encodeURIComponent(id);\nsurname = document.getElementById(elementId).value;\n```\n\nIn this version, the `encodeURIComponent` function is used to encode the `id` variable, ensuring that it cannot be used to inject malicious code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7b4e5d38-189e-34d1-8525-cc834f0f27df",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the fetch API is used to send a POST request. The fetch API returns a Promise that resolves to the Response to that request, whether it is successful or not. If the request fails due to network issues, or if any other error occurs while issuing the request, the Promise is rejected. However, the provided code does not include a catch block to handle such rejections, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include a catch block when using Promises to handle any potential rejections. The catch block should include appropriate error handling logic.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\nfetch('change_user_details.php', {\n\tmethod: 'POST',\n\theaders: {\n\t\t'Accept': 'application/json',\n\t\t'Content-Type': 'application/json'\n\t},\n\tbody: JSON.stringify({ 'id': id, 'first_name': first_name, 'surname': surname })\n})\n.then((response) => response.json())\n.catch((error) => {\n\t// Handle the error here\n\tconsole.error('Error:', error);\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code uses the Fetch API, which is built into modern browsers and does not require any additional library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ea7c8411-9dbf-3eb9-9593-fbb32032a2a7",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in `cell0`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. In this case, the vulnerability arises from the fact that user-controlled data (`user['user_id']`) is directly used in the `innerHTML` property of `cell0` without any sanitization or encoding. This allows an attacker to inject arbitrary HTML or JavaScript code, which will be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always treat user input as untrusted data. You should use context-specific output encoding/escaping techniques on all user-supplied data before including it in HTML output. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix would be to use `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` when setting the text of the `cell0` element. This ensures that the user input is treated as plain text and not parsed as HTML. \n\n```javascript\ncell0.textContent = user['user_id'];\ncell0.innerHTML += '<input type=\"hidden\" id=\"user_id_' + user['user_id'] + '\" name=\"user_id\" value=\"' + user['user_id'] + '\" />';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet does not appear to have any specific library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0afceef3-d05c-3cf4-9dd6-545b67ab656a",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in `cell1`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the XSS vulnerability arises from the user-controlled data in `cell1`. The `innerHTML` property is being used to add HTML code to `cell1`. However, the user's ID and first name are being directly inserted into the HTML string without any sanitization or encoding. If an attacker can control the `user_id` or `first_name` values, they could inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use the principle of least privilege. Only allow necessary permissions for the user.\n- Validate and sanitize user input. Never trust user input.\n- Use output encoding libraries to ensure that the output is encoded to the context in which it is being inserted into the HTML.\n- Use appropriate response headers to prevent XSS in HTTP responses.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nA simple fix for this specific vulnerability would be to use `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` when inserting user-controlled data into the HTML. This ensures that the user-controlled data is treated as text and not as HTML code.\n\n```javascript\nlet input = document.createElement('input');\ninput.type = 'text';\ninput.id = 'first_name_' + user['user_id'];\ninput.name = 'first_name';\ninput.value = user['first_name'];\ncell1.appendChild(input);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c6ed0a89-b76b-3401-9cfa-96e01a5d277a",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerability Risk in User-Controlled Data within `cell2.innerHTML`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection security vulnerability where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into webpages viewed by other users. In this case, the vulnerability arises from the fact that the `innerHTML` property is being used to directly insert user-controlled data (`user['user_id']` and `user['surname']`) into the HTML content of `cell2`. If an attacker can control the `user_id` or `surname` values, they can inject malicious scripts that will be executed when the HTML content is rendered by a web browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid directly inserting user-controlled data into HTML content. Instead, use text content property or methods that automatically encode HTML entities. If you must insert user-controlled data into HTML content, ensure that the data is properly sanitized and encoded to prevent the execution of any injected scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer way to create the HTML content would be to create the elements using the DOM API, which automatically encodes HTML entities:\n\n```javascript\nlet input = document.createElement('input');\ninput.type = 'text';\ninput.id = 'surname_' + user['user_id'];\ninput.name = 'surname';\ninput.value = user['surname'];\ncell2.appendChild(input);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
